TI  - An amplification-free dual-readout biosensor integrating colorimetry and 
      single-particle counting for ultrasensitive miRNA detection in esophageal cancer.
PG  - 118947
LID - S0956-5663(26)00579-8 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.bios.2026.118947 [doi]
AB  - Esophageal cancer (EC) remains highly lethal partly because many patients are 
      diagnosed at advanced stages, and current diagnostic pathways rely on invasive 
      endoscopy and centralized molecular testing. Circulating microRNAs (miRNAs) are 
      promising minimally invasive biomarkers, yet amplification-based assays can be 
      workflow-intensive and introduce protocol-dependent bias. Here we report an 
      amplification-free, dual-readout gold nanoparticle (AuNP) biosensor for 
      hsa-miR-320b analysis in plasma RNA extracts. Target miR-320b bridges a 
      biotinylated capture probe on streptavidin magnetic beads and a thiolated 
      detection probe on 40-nm AuNPs to form sandwich complexes. A shared 
      magnetic-separation workflow enables two sequential readouts: a colorimetric 
      verification based on AuNP depletion in the supernatant (LOD 0.085 pM) and a 
      digital release-to-count (R2C) mode, in which RNase H selectively cleaves the RNA 
      strand within the DNA/RNA heteroduplex to release AuNP-DNA labels for dark-field 
      single-particle counting (LOD 5.3 fM) without nucleic-acid amplification. The 
      assay demonstrates single-base mismatch discrimination, short-term storage 
      stability, and spike recoveries of 89.8 -92.9% in plasma RNA extracts. In a 
      cohort of 60 clinical plasma samples (20 EC, 20 benign lesions, 20 healthy 
      controls), the R2C digital readout showed an EC-associated elevation of plasma 
      miR-320b relative to benign lesions and healthy controls, with trends consistent 
      with reverse transcription quantitative PCR (RT-qPCR) on the same extracts. This 
      work provides a practical route to amplification-free digital miRNA 
      quantification using countable plasmonic labels and is readily extended to other 
      circulating targets.
