DP  - 2026 Jun 18
TI  - Mineral Content and Bioactive Potential of Quince (Cydonia oblonga) Peels for 
      Value-Added Food Production.
LID - 10.1007/s11130-026-01528-7 [doi]
LID - 78
AB  - The food sector is resource-intensive and under pressure to reduce its 
      environmental footprint, with by-product valorization offering a sustainable 
      solution. This study evaluated the potential of quince peels, a major processing 
      by-product, for innovative food applications. Nine quince varieties from an 
      organic farm in the Czech Republic were analyzed over three years. Elemental 
      composition was determined by AAS, total polyphenols and antioxidant activity 
      spectrophotometrically, and phenolics by HPLC. Quince peels were rich in K 
      (4096-8068 mg/kg), Ca (701-1789 mg/kg), Mg (447-1201 mg/kg), and P 
      (588-1544 mg/kg), with low Na content.The contents of Cu and Zn ranged from 3.2 
      to 9.6 and 6.9-27.2 mg/kg, respectively. Also, levels of Mn, Fe, Cr, Ni and Co 
      (0.9-2.6; 17.7-56.1; 0.1-1.0; 0.1-2.1 and < LOD - 0.8 mg/kg, respectively) were 
      remarkable. The contents of Pb and Cd were below the limit of detection in most 
      samples. The average total phenolic content in quince peels was 13.82 mg GAE/g 
      DW. Antioxidant activity varied depending on the assay, with high reducing power 
      (FRAP), moderate radical scavenging activity (DPPH), and lower ABTS values. On 
      the other hand, quince peels are an excellent source of chlorogenic acid (5-CQA) 
      which average value was 2870 mg/kg DW, and its derivatives, such as 
      neochlorogenic acid (3-CQA), cryptochlorogenic acid (4-CQA) as well as 
      3,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid (3,5-DCQA). The surprising component of the quince peel 
      was rutin (average content 3940 mg/kg DW). These findings suggest that quince 
      peels represent a promising ingredient for the development of functional food 
      products, particularly as natural mineral and antioxidant-rich additives in 
      bakery products, fruit-based preparations, and meat formulations.
