TI  - Continuous Hydrogen Sulfide Monitor Based on Positive Fluorescence Green 
      Chemistry. Application to Geothermal Powerplant Environmental Impact Assessment.
PG  - 26993-27001
LID - 10.1021/acsomega.6c00561 [doi]
AB  - Renewable geothermal sources can be used for base-load power generation. Prior 
      environmental impact assessment is needed, however, for H(2)S emitted from wells 
      and cooling towers. Detailed spatiotemporal H(2)S concentration data need 
      continuous monitoring by sensitive and fieldable H(2)S analyzers. Fluorescein 
      mercuric acetate (FMA), a toxic organomercuric reagent, has been a staple for 
      H(2)S measurement. We report a reagent containing copper 
      8-hydroxyquinoline-5-sulfonic acid (HQS) complex and Zn(2+). As HS(-) from sample 
      air bearing H(2)S reacts with such a reagent, thiophilic copper forms the 
      hydrosulfide, and the released HQS forms a fluorescent zinc chelate. An automated 
      fieldable H(2)S analyzer based on this green chemistry, comprising a disposable 
      gas scrubber with controlled liquid flow at 50 muL/min and a UV-laser based 
      fluorescence detector, is reported. The instrument operates on a (4 min sample, 6 
      min zero) reporting the difference signal via a Raspberry Pi controller to the 
      cloud every 10 min with a working range of 0.3 ppb (limit of detection) to >100 
      ppb. During each cycle a total of 0.8 L air and 1 mL aqueous solution containing 
      5 mumol NaOH, 10 mumol 4-(2-hydroxyethyl)-1-piperazineethanesulfonic acid, 40 nmol 
      HQS, and 20 nmol each of Cu and Zn are consumed with a total chemical cost < USD 
      $0.01. Performance characteristics and data from a geothermal field are reported. 
      It provides far superior sensitivity and time resolution than conventional 
      impinger absorption and colorimetry. It should find wide use in geothermal 
      environmental assessment and for monitoring oil drilling and storage sites, 
      paper-pulp and sewage treatment facilities, livestock management, etc.
