TI  - Employment of mixed aptamers and gold nanobipyramids toward a paper-based 
      aptasensor for simultaneously visual screening and accurate detection of eight 
      sulfonamides.
PG  - 127574
LID - S1386-1425(26)00145-9 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.saa.2026.127574 [doi]
AB  - A variety of sulfonamides (SAs) are extensively utilized in animal husbandry and 
      aquaculture, necessitating the development of high-throughput methods for the 
      rapid screening and accurate determination of multiple, ideally all 
      commonly-applied SAs, in animal-derived foodstuffs. We herein developed a 
      paper-based colorimetric aptasensor for the detection of eight different SAs 
      employing a mixture of two broad-specific aptamers as bio-receptor, ascorbic acid 
      (AA)-mediated gold nanobipyramids (AuNBPs) growth for generating color signals, 
      and a paper-based analytical device (PAD) for stabilizing and enhancing color 
      signals. The aptasensor exhibits target SAs concentration-corresponding multiple 
      color changes and has excellent specificity. It can be used to visually screen 
      eight SAs (either individually or collectively) with a visual detection limit 
      (LOD) of 4.0 muM, and quantitatively detect these SAs (either individually or 
      collectively) with a spectrometry LOD of 0.2 muM. Importantly, the signals of the 
      aptasensor can be stably stored for one month for later verification, and the 
      spectrometry LOD is lower than the maximum residue limit (MRL) of total SAs in 
      animal-derived foodstuffs. The aptasensor was successfully applied to detect 
      eight SAs (either individually or collectively) in milk samples with a recovery 
      of 89% - 95% and a relative standard deviation (RSD, n = 5) < 8%. The results 
      obtained with the aptasensor were consistent with those obtained using commercial 
      ELISA kit. These findings demonstrated that the aptasensor simultaneously meets 
      the requirement of instrument-free screening and quantitative determination of 
      eight SAs in animal-derived foods, making it a promising approach for ensuring 
      the consumption safety of animal-derived foodstuffs.
