TI  - Fully Self-Powered Gas Sensor through Fe-Ion Doping Engineering in Ni(2)P for 
      Ultrasensitive and Visualized NO(2) Sensing.
PG  - 716-727
LID - 10.1021/acssensors.5c03884 [doi]
AB  - Self-powered gas sensors based on zinc-air batteries (ZABs) integrate battery and 
      gas sensing functions. Various gaseous reduction reactions at the battery cathode 
      will induce a measurable and reproducible change in the battery's output voltage, 
      eliminating the need for any external power supply. However, they still face 
      significant challenges in achieving ultrasensitive, selective, and 
      drift-resistant sensing under complex conditions. Herein, we report a NO(2) gas 
      sensor based on a ZAB, utilizing Fe-doped nickel phosphide (FNP) as the 
      gas-sensitive material and incorporating deep learning algorithms to enhance the 
      sensing performance. In the context of FNP gas-sensitive layers, the charge 
      carrier mobility is significantly enhanced, owing to the electron delocalization 
      and redistribution induced by the Fe dopants. Furthermore, the shift in the 
      d-band center toward the Fermi level induced by Fe dopants facilitates stronger O 
      2p/Fe d orbital hybridization. As a result, the adsorbate-substrate interaction 
      is enhanced and the Gibbs free energy of the NO(2) reduction reaction is reduced. 
      The charge density difference also indicates the facilitated electron transfer 
      from FNP to NO(2) and the electron accumulation at the O sites. Consequently, the 
      sensors exhibit a high response (0.22 V @ 20 ppm), a low limit of detection (LOD: 
      61.8 ppb), and fast sensing speed (14 s). After that, the introduction of the 
      InceptionTime model and wavelet transformation algorithm enables the sensor to 
      achieve remarkable gas recognition and concentration quantification, along with a 
      significantly reduced LOD of 36.9 ppb. Finally, a smart sensing device is 
      constructed with the sensors, microcontrollers, and electrochromic devices for 
      remote and visualized gas detections.
FAU - Sun, Zhaokun
