TI  - Development of flame frequency stabilized-continuous wave laser excited atomic 
      fluorescence spectrometric (flame-cw-LEAFS) technique and its applications for 
      ultra-trace level determination of rubidium in spodumene.
PG  - 129243
LID - S0039-9140(25)01734-5 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.talanta.2025.129243 [doi]
AB  - A frequency-stabilized continuous wave laser-excited atomic fluorescence 
      spectrometry (flame-cw-LEAFS) method has been developed in an air-acetylene flame 
      for ultra-trace determination of rubidium (Rb) in spodumene and related matrices. 
      In contrast to conventional wavelength-scan LEAFS, which suffers from frequency 
      drifts, laser power fluctuations, high sample consumption, and long acquisition 
      times, the present approach stabilizes a cw-ring Ti:sapphire (Ti:Sa) laser to the 
      Doppler-broadened hyperfine component of the (85)Rb D2 transition. Stabilization 
      was achieved through frequency modulation of the intracavity Brewster plate and 
      phase-sensitive detection of Rb fluorescence in a reference cell. Approximately 
      100 mW power of the stabilized laser output was directed into a modified 
      commercial air-acetylene flame atomizer, and resonance fluorescence was collected 
      orthogonally with a photomultiplier tube and narrow-band interference filter. 
      Optimization of laser power, beam geometry, and collection optics suppressed 
      scattering and maximized the signal-to-noise ratio. Under optimized conditions, 
      the method achieved a limit of detection of  approximately 0.8 ng mL(-1), a linear dynamic 
      range of 5-250 ng mL(-1), and precision of 1 % RSD at 50 ng mL(-1). Long-term 
      stability studies showed wavelength fluctuations of only  approximately 3 fm over 1200 s of 
      closed-loop operation. Accuracy of the developed analytical technique was 
      confirmed by analysis of NIST SRMs and cross-validation with graphite furnace AAS 
      (GFAAS), while spike recoveries from aqueous and spodumene digests exceeded 90 %. 
      These results demonstrate that flame frequency-stabilized-cw-LEAFS provides a 
      sensitive, precise, and practical alternative to conventional AAS and GFAAS, 
      while avoiding the complexity and cost of ICP-MS, making it a promising tool for 
      trace alkali metal analysis in complex matrices.
